[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Converting the fleet design corpus to sdoc

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-CONVERT-IS-ADJUDICATION
TITLE: Converting the fleet corpus is adjudication, not migration
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The corpus is fully converted, at which point there is nothing left to adjudicate.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The source corpus is not trusted input. Its author could not manage it and does
not know what is stale, what is dirty, or which requirements survive from
superseded designs. Therefore no bulk conversion is planned. A cluster is
converted at the moment someone is about to work on it, and the act of
converting it is the act of deciding what is still true.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A migration project would import the mess wholesale and produce a validated
graph of assertions nobody has checked. The parser proves an edge resolves; it
cannot prove the two ends agree.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-VIEWER-BEFORE-BULK
TITLE: The viewer ships before any bulk conversion
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: superseded
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A derived human-readable view exists and someone reads a converted slice from
it without opening a raw sdoc file.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Until a derived view exists, converting prose to sdoc makes the reading surface
worse, not better. Source that was narrative becomes UID and STATEMENT blocks,
and StrictDoc's own renders are out of scope by choice.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The corpus was graded F for human digestion after reading ten percent of it.
Conversion contributes nothing to that grade on its own. Converting the whole
corpus before building the viewer spends weeks and leaves the grade standing.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
This is the strongest argument against the whole approach and is recorded as an
open decision rather than buried, so it has to be answered rather than forgotten.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-HITL-IS-THE-VIEWER
  ROLE: Superseded_By

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-FIRST-CONVERSION
TITLE: First conversion target is the lane-iteration cluster
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Convert 12-flow-iterate, 13-flow-settle and 14-flow-kill. 426 lines, roughly 60
nodes: one SLICE, ~26 MECHANISM, 13 INVARIANT from the grain rules, ~15
DECISION, ~4 SPIKE.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Measured as the only multi-file design cluster carrying zero references to the
fleet-shape hinge, which is the corpus's largest instability with 22
localizations across 20 files. 14-flow-kill states outright that the hinge does
not reopen the flow. Eight dead out-edges against eighteen for the nearest
alternative, and six of the eight become live once register rows are nodes.
Two of its own open items dissolve rather than transfer if it is built host-side
first, which the corpus already offers as an escape hatch.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-CONVERT-IS-ADJUDICATION:0000000
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-CONVERT-IS-ADJUDICATION
  ROLE: Governed_By
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-HITL-IS-THE-VIEWER
  ROLE: Assumes

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DROPPED-SECTION-TOMBSTONES
TITLE: Tombstone the nine dropped sections before converting past the first slice
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Create nine DECISION nodes with STATUS superseded, one per dropped section, each
recording its pending citation count and retiring on the first slice that needs
the content restored.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
123 references across 9 dropped sections would otherwise become 123 hard export
errors at once, and resolving them means deciding whether four subsystems are
restored or their citations rewritten. That is a design decision on four
subsystems gated behind a tooling migration, and it is where the conversion
would stall. Tombstones convert a hard block into nine tracked debts. The first
slice needs only two of them.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-CONVERT-IS-ADJUDICATION:0000000
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-CONVERT-IS-ADJUDICATION
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-DERIVE-CACHED-LAYERS
TITLE: Delete the hand-maintained summary layers and derive them from JSON
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Delete the index table, the grain index, and the per-file frontmatter counters.
Regenerate each from the JSON export.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
These are the duplicated cache layer that motivated the move. They are already
drifting: four of forty-one files disagree with their own bodies, and the grain
index covers 16 of 127 rules while reading as complete. This is the one part of
the reading problem that conversion fixes mechanically on day one.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-CONVERT-IS-ADJUDICATION:0000000
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-CONVERT-IS-ADJUDICATION
  ROLE: Governed_By

[SPIKE]
UID: SPIKE-CONVERSION-SIGNAL
TITLE: Does converting 426 lines surface an unknown contradiction
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: unrun
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first slice is converted and either surfaces at least one contradiction the
author did not know about, or does not.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Convert the lane-iteration cluster and count contradictions discovered that were
not known beforehand. A null result falsifies the approach for the cost of one
day.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A fresh reader found several in an afternoon without looking hard, so a null
result would be surprising and therefore informative.
<<<
