[DECISION]
UID: DEC-HITL-IS-THE-VIEWER
TITLE: An LLM renders for human reading until a viewer exists
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A working beads sandbox exists and the viewer that follows it is in use.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Human reading is served by on-the-fly LLM expansion during a working session,
not by a rendered artifact. The operator makes decisions in conversation, and
the model pulls in whatever neighbouring content the decision needs.

Conversion is therefore not gated on a viewer. Order of work is the sdoc plan,
then early beads implementation, then the viewer.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Readability is not the near-term bottleneck. Deciding what is safe to build is.
The single-source-of-truth property and well-defined edges to that truth are
what matter in the early hours; the render is downstream of both and can wait.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-VIEWER-FORM-UNDECIDED
TITLE: The viewer's form is not chosen
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The kanban TUI prototype spike concludes, which is expected around the time the
sdoc work lands.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Whether the eventual viewer is a terminal client or a web view is open. A kanban
TUI prototype with a generic backend is in progress on the side and its outcome
is the deciding input.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded so the choice is made on the prototype's evidence rather than by
whichever surface gets built first.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-RENDER-CACHE
TITLE: Human-readable renders are cached on the fingerprint of their inputs
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
A rendered chunk is produced by a model, not deterministically: it expands one
node and pulls in neighbouring content that adds value to the reader. Because
generation costs tokens, each rendered chunk records the set of nodes that fed
it and a fingerprint over their contract fields. The chunk is regenerated only
when one of those inputs changes.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is the same primitive as the suspect-link check, pointed at a different
question. Suspect detection asks whether a node's parent contract moved since it
was accepted; render caching asks whether any input to a chunk moved since it
was drawn. Both hash the contract fields of a node set and compare. Building the
fingerprint machinery therefore serves the viewer as well as drift detection,
which is an argument for doing it before either consumer needs it.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-HITL-IS-THE-VIEWER:0000000
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
